                     Imperium - ImpBot, the computer player

ImpBot is a computer player. It connects to TelImp's TCP port and plays by
typing commands and reading what the game prints, exactly as a person at a
terminal does. It is not part of the server and changes nothing in it: it
sees only what its own scans show it, and it is held to the same BTUs,
money, production and time limits as every other player.

    ImpBot -w PASSWORD [-h HOST] [-p PORT] [-n NAME] [-c CREATION-PASSWORD]
           [-l easy|normal|hard] [-r RACE] [-s STATEFILE] [-L LOGFILE] [-1]

    -h HOST      the game's host (default 127.0.0.1)
    -p PORT      TelImp's port (default 3458)
    -n NAME      the player's name; it is created the first time
    -w PASSWORD  the player's password (at most 9 characters)
    -c PASSWORD  the game's creation password (default add.me)
    -l LEVEL     how it plays: easy, normal (the default) or hard
    -r RACE      the race to join; otherwise the one with fewest players
    -s FILE      where it keeps what it has learnt (its map, the planets it
                 has seen) between sessions
    -L FILE      a log of everything it typed and read
    -1           play one session and stop

It plays in sessions: it logs in, spends its BTUs, logs off, and comes back
when they have built up again. In a session it

  - reads its ships and planets (ships, census), and sets each new planet
    to work (divvy): a young empire keeps a good share for cash so it
    never runs out of money, and as its money builds up it turns its
    planets to ore, technology and the parts of warships, and back again
    if money runs short;
  - explores with its ships, one scan at a time, and remembers the map;
  - scans planets it reaches, settles those with air, and briefly holds
    those without to scan the sectors around from them;
  - builds torpedo tubes on its planets and installs them, so its planets
    fire back when attacked, with more on a planet that has been fired on
    and more again once it has money to spare;
  - builds new ships and fits them with an engine, computer, life support,
    sensors and, when it can, a torpedo tube; it runs more ships as its
    money and planets grow;
  - makes fuel tanks on every planet it settles and keeps its ships
    fuelled from them;
  - leaves its five officers on its first colony, and if that is a
    small planet starts an officer school there: the planet trains
    soldiers while it still has few people, then officers (the game
    trains officers only where soldiers are more than 3% of the most
    people a planet can hold, with officers at least 5% of them);
    officers are carried to its warships first and then to planets with
    torpedo tubes, which need one to fire;
  - gives up a colony that is being fired on and has almost nobody left,
    taking its officers away rather than sending it more;
  - and, when it judges itself strong enough, sends armed ships against
    the planets of its neighbours (fire).

If its player is still marked as logged on after a connection broke, it
logs on anyway, as a person would.

The levels differ in how much they spend and how bold they are:

    easy    keeps BTUs and cash in reserve, builds little, and only strikes
            back at whoever has attacked it (it learns who from its
            telegrams)
    normal  spends most of its BTUs, settles a dozen or so planets, and
            attacks once it has two armed ships and three planets (or one
            armed ship and five planets); a crewed warship with no enemy
            in sight goes looking at planets nobody has examined
    hard    spends everything, settles widely, arms more, and attacks
            with its first armed ship

A computer empire left with no ships and no planets starts again as a new
player (its name with II, III or IV after it), as a person might.

Run it with a deity's permission, like any other player: it takes one of
the game's player places.
